<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="prep-2-15">
  <title>Resources for 2/15</title>

  <objectives>
    <introduction>
      <p>For the content of this class, you will be expected to be able to do the following:</p>
    </introduction>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <p>
          Given two elements in a <em>partially ordered set (i.e. poset)</em>, determine whether or not they are <em>comparable</em>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain the difference between a <em>minimum</em> and a <em>minimal</em> element in a partially ordered set.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Explain the difference between a <em>maximum</em> and a <em>maximal</em> element in a partially ordered set.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          Given a partially ordered set, determine any minimum, minimal, maximum, or maximal elements of the partially ordered set and prove the result.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <subsection xml:id="video-activity-comparability-max-min">
    <title>Video &amp; Activities for Comparability &amp; Maximum/Minimum Elements</title>
    <p>
      Below is are two YouTube videos on <em>comparability</em> and concepts related to <em>maximum and minimum elements</em>. It is followed by a link to a set of activities that we will be working on in class. Please watch the video and do as many of the activities as you can before class.
    </p>

    <sidebyside widths="45% 45%">
      <video youtube="IZzOgiYB4o4" />
      <video youtube="IZzOgiYB4o4" />
    </sidebyside>

    <problem>
      <title>Partial Orders - Directed Graph Model</title>
      <p>
      Solve the following partial order problems.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Let <m>A = \mathbb{R} \times \mathbb{R}</m> (i.e. the set of points on the Cartesian plane).  We define the relation <m>R</m> on <m>A</m> by <m>(a,b) R (c,d)</m> when <m>a \le c</m> and <m>b \le d</m>.
              <ol>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Prove that <m>R</m> is a partial order.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Prove that <m>R</m> is <em>not</em> a total order (i.e. it is not connex).
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Define a relation <m>R</m> on the set of vertices of the figure below by <m>xRy</m> precisely when <m>x \rightarrow y</m>.
            </p>
            <image width="60%">
              <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="image-prep-2-partial-order-2">
                <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
                  <definition>relation={1:[2],2:[4],3:[2]}</definition>
                  <definition>labels={1:'a',2:'b',3:'c',4:'d'}</definition>
                  <coordinates bbox="(0,0,1.04,1)">
                    <network directed="yes" graph="relation" labels="yes" label-dictionary="labels"
                    scale="0.8" node-stroke="black" node-fill="white" >
                      <node at="1" p="(0.2,0.8)" />
                      <node at="2" p="(0.8,0.8)" />
                      <node at="3" p="(0.2,0.2)" />
                      <node at="4" p="(0.8,0.2)" />
                    </network>
                  </coordinates>
                </diagram>
              </prefigure>
            </image>
            <p>
              <ol>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Is this a partial order?  Why or why not?
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    What arrows would have to be added to make this a partial order?  Go ahead and add them.
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    What pairs of elements are comparable in the relation you created in (b)?
                  </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                  <p>
                    Identify all maximum, minimum, maximal, and minimal elements of the partial order you created in (b).
                  </p>
                </li>
              </ol>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Do the same as #2 with the following figure.
            </p>
            <image width="60%">
              <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="image-prep-2-partial-order-3">
                <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
                  <definition>relation={1:[2],3:[4,5,6],4:[1,2],5:[2],6:[1,5]}</definition>
                  <definition>labels={1:'a',2:'b',3:'c',4:'d',5:'e',6:'f'}</definition>
                  <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,1,1)">
                    <network directed="yes" graph="relation" node-fill="white" labels="yes" 
                    label-dictionary="labels" scale="0.8" seed="1" />
                  </coordinates>
                </diagram>
              </prefigure>
            </image>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Can the following figure have arrows added to make it a partial order?  Why or why not?
            </p>
            <image width="60%">
              <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="image-prep-2-partial-order-4">
                <diagram dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
                  <definition>relation={1:[2,3,5],2:[5],3:[4],4:[1,2,5]}</definition>
                  <definition>labels={1:'a',2:'b',3:'c',4:'d',5:'e',6:'f'}</definition>
                  <coordinates bbox="(-1,-1,1,1)">
                    <network directed="yes" graph="relation" node-fill="white" labels="yes" 
                    label-dictionary="labels" scale="0.8" seed="9" />
                  </coordinates>
                </diagram>
              </prefigure>
            </image>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </problem>
    <problem>
      <title>Partial Orders - Divisibility Relation</title>
      <p>
      Define the relation <m>R</m> on <m>{\mathbb N}</m> (where <m>\mathbb{N}</m> includes 0) by <m>a R b</m> precisely when <m>a</m> divides evenly into <m>b</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Prove that this is a partial order.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Find two elements of <m>\mathbb{N}</m> that are comparable, and find two elements that are not comparable.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Identify all maximum, minimum, maximal, and minimal elements.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              (Not on quiz) Do #3 when the set is <m>P=\{ a \in \mathbb{N} : a \ge 2\}</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </problem>
  </subsection>

  <subsection>
    <title>Optional Reading</title>
    <p>
      <url href="">Partial Orders and Partially Ordered Sets</url>
    </p>
  </subsection>
</section>